WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:03.340
This is Enceladus –

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:05.760
Saturn’s sixth largest moon.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:08.400
At around 500 km in diameter,

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.060
Enceladus is encased in a crust of ice

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:13.240
up to 40 km thick--

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:16.140
floating atop a vast salty ocean.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:17.960
From afar, Enceladus looks like a

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:19.320
smooth, white pearl,

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:20.980
but a closer look at its surface

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:23.380
reveals a rougher reality.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.920
Its north pole is riddled with impact craters,

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.300
scars left over from run-ins with asteroids

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:30.460
and other cosmic debris.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:31.800
On Enceladus’ south pole,

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.080
we see long, aquamarine
“tiger stripes” --

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:36.080
fractures in the ice

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:38.760
that spew tall plumes of water vapor and ice particles

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:41.540
kilometers above the surface.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:42.900
Since 2005,

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:44.960
Cassini has been gathering and analyzing

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:47.020
material from these plumes.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:48.680
We know they contain water vapor

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:50.880
and traces
of nitrogen and methane,

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:51.820
but the current mission

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:54.040
was to look for molecular hydrogen.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:55.100
Here on Earth,

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:57.240
organisms known as chemolithotrophs

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.100
have the ability to harvest chemical energy

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:00.300
from hydrogen--

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:01.460
forming a base of life

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:03.740
independent of sunlight.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:05.180
In this latest plume dive,

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:07.960
Cassini screamed
past Enceladus’ south pole

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:10.400
at around 8.5 km/sec,

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:12.660
a mere 50 km above the surface

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:14.480
at its lowest point.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:16.260
Gas captured during the flyby

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:17.640
revealed that these plumes

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.980
do contain hydrogen gas

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:22.460
This suggests that Enceladus’ rocky core

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:24.360
is interacting with the warm waters

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.900
of the subsurface ocean.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:27.740
Such an interaction

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:30.040
makes Enceladus a more hospitable environment

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:32.900
for life than any other body in the solar system

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:34.960
--outside of Earth

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:36.460
This was, unfortunately,

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:38.720
one of Cassini’s last missions.

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:40.440
NASA will soon send the satellite

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:42.100
on a series of 22 dives

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:44.080
between the planet and its rings,

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:46.860
a region of Saturn yet to be explored.

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:48.280
These dangerous dives will reveal

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:49.320
new information about

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.220
the formation of Saturn's rings--

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:53.020
are they as old as the solar system

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:55.200
or a new addition?

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:56.860
During its last five orbits,

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:59.900
Cassini will dip a toe into Saturn’s atmosphere.

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:00.700
In September,

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:02.700
Cassini is set to plunge into
Saturn--

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:04.250
sending back what measurements it

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:06.240
can before it vaporizes.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.940
Bringing to a close a 20 year exploration

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:12.420
of one our solar system’s largest residents.

